42
00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:05.969
Stefano Gogioso: selling points. Just a brief recap of where we started. This is a proposal for an emergency mitigation.

43
00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:24.449
Stefano Gogioso: for a quantum break, an unexpected quantum break, so something that happens without any real notice, and before we can finish our transition. There are some ziderata. The key ones are that this should work on hardware wallets today. It should not force users to

44
00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:31.770
Stefano Gogioso: relinquished security for their holdings, and that it should be a solution that works

45
00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:54.870
Stefano Gogioso: after the emergency has become apparent, so it can't require user action prior to an emergency. So it's something which has to be a recovery, in a sense, not just a pre-planning in case something goes wrong, in the presumption that most users wouldn't actually believe this is necessary, and so they wouldn't take action. So it's a way to protect everybody and recover in case something unexpected happens.

46
00:06:54.930 --> 00:07:01.640
Stefano Gogioso: And the question was, can this be done compatibly with, a bunch of constraints? One of them is, of course, hardware wallets.

47
00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:16.980
Stefano Gogioso: But the general idea, the general plan comes from a proposal by Vitalik in 2024, which is to, perform what is known as proof lifting. This is all set up.

48
00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:22.499
Stefano Gogioso: In a paper just maybe a few weeks, concurrent to the 2024 proposal.

49
00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:26.720
Stefano Gogioso: And the… the idea is to lift

50
00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:40.990
Stefano Gogioso: the weak part, so the ECDSA, to some quantum-resistant scheme by taking a zero-knowledge proof of a hard-to-invert pre-image, so a preimage of a hash step in the key derivation.

51
00:07:40.990 --> 00:08:02.069
Stefano Gogioso: and then use account obstruction or other delegation features to actually derive authority on-chain from the zero-knowledge proof of seed. So you do the lifting by essentially sacrificing or proving that you are the legitimate owner of your account, and then you operate through account obstruction, so you don't operate through the EOA anymore.

52
00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:06.099
Stefano Gogioso: And this talk is really about step one, and a good

53
00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:24.509
Stefano Gogioso: amount of progress on Step 2. I think mostly it's account abstraction itself, which is not particularly hard to do, but it's the feasibility of the lifting statements and CHAIN Today, which is… which still requires some work. But I will present some, some estimates for what… what we could implement and test.

54
00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:41.750
Stefano Gogioso: And the focus is on hierarchical deterministic wallets, so mostly statements about child keys from parent keys, the master key can be lifted to the master seat, and the master seat itself can, in principle, be lifted all the way to the seed phrase bytes, or intermediate stages if they are exposed.

55
00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:51.780
Stefano Gogioso: There are other key generation schemes that have similar properties. If your key generation scheme doesn't have that property, then it's probably unsalvable anyway.

56
00:08:51.850 --> 00:09:02.230
Stefano Gogioso: But… because it could be just that you have the private key randomly generated, and that's broken, and then you can… you have no other way of proving that you generated that, other than you rolled some dice.

57
00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:17.770
Stefano Gogioso: But many schemes have this kind of sort of deterministic or pseudo-random generation, and those can… they're typically hash-based, and therefore they typically have a hard-to-invert stat that can be used for lifting. So it's more than just deterministic wallets, but that's what we focused on.

58
00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:32.109
Stefano Gogioso: The main challenge for this project was to make it work on hardware wallets, and hardware wallets are extremely constrained devices, memory constrained. The solution was to use a technique that's not succinct.

59
00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:47.629
Stefano Gogioso: But is very, contained in its memory requirements, so this is stream the NPC in the head on the secure element, so on the hardware wallet, and then use recursive verification outside of the hardware wallet.

60
00:09:47.630 --> 00:10:00.519
Stefano Gogioso: to provide the on-chain verification feasibility. The status today is that we do run all of this on device, everything fits, everything has been optimized to the point where it is realistic to do this.

61
00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:20.360
Stefano Gogioso: We do have 3 lifters implemented. We have Fry plus Blocky, we have SP1 and FLOC. Really, SP1 and FLOC are the ones that would be affordable on L1 today, but nonetheless, we have figures for, like, cost… like, gas estimates for all of them, and roughly also performance estimates and memory estimates for the prover side of things.

62
00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:29.070
Stefano Gogioso: The other challenge is that lifting child keys to master, sort of ancestor keys or master key is fine, it can be done on device.

63
00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:52.360
Stefano Gogioso: So, on a stock ledger today, but if you have a compromised master key or a compromised seed, which you shouldn't in practical applications, but, you know, it's still interesting to consider, then you'd need access to secure OS internals. The current, the stock ledger OS does not expose the necessary information, so that would require additional integration to be actually implemented.

64
00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:54.799
Stefano Gogioso: Nonetheless, we did this with mock

65
00:10:55.120 --> 00:11:09.780
Stefano Gogioso: with a mock pre-image on device to test that it would run, and indeed it does run. So it is feasible from a hardware perspective, it's just a limitation of what secure OS exposes, and it would require further integration to be actually implemented. But it is technically feasible.

66
00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:29.849
Stefano Gogioso: Statements that we considered, these are the ones that are relevant to Ethereum. There are also Solana and Bitcoin statements that we considered and implemented and benchmarked. The master private key has the master seat as witness, the hardened child keys have the parent key as witness.

67
00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:39.710
Stefano Gogioso: and the BIP39 seed has the mnemonic and technically passphrases witness, and none of these require, elliptic curve.

68
00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:56.839
Stefano Gogioso: cryptography. So, that makes a difference for us, because secp256k1 is THE elephant in the room. It's the thing that makes it very hard to prove these things. You'll see the size of the proofs is, like, a thousand-fold, something like that. 500-fold.

69
00:11:56.840 --> 00:12:00.360
Stefano Gogioso: Because of all these operations, that need to be arithmetized.

70
00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:05.519
Stefano Gogioso: No, actually, not a RTMS, that need to be put into the MCP net.

71
00:12:06.190 --> 00:12:19.179
Stefano Gogioso: The public key statement, the non-hardened child key statements, and the ethereum address, which is really just a public key statement, those require this sort of elephant element, and they are harder to prove.

72
00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:29.830
Stefano Gogioso: everything can be proved on stock ledger without secure OS access, except for the master private key, because the master seat is not exposed, and the seat itself, because the mnemonic is not exposed.

73
00:12:29.830 --> 00:12:33.340
Stefano Gogioso: But if you integrate it with a secure device, in principle, this could be proved as well.

74
00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:38.690
Stefano Gogioso: what did we do since EthCC, which is the last time we presented this?

75
00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:41.010
Stefano Gogioso: We…

76
00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:58.309
Stefano Gogioso: made it so that the prover runs on Ledger Flex in user app space, so we have Ledger Flex applications that have been, like, tested and benchmarked, and we proved all six statements and verified them on device. We reached full soundness on hardware for the hardened

77
00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:10.680
Stefano Gogioso: for the hardened child key statement, which is the interesting one in practice, it's about 22 megabytes, and it takes about 3 hours to generate 438 responses.

78
00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:24.669
Stefano Gogioso: The elephant statements, so the elliptic curve statements, have been optimized about 350 times, 350X, sorry, since the April, and can now run on device in a few days, so that's also realistic, although a leaf slower.

79
00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:39.949
Stefano Gogioso: we did… we built 3 succeed liftings for the Stage 2, so one is Stark, one is SP1, and one is FLOC. And we did full-on change verification for the SP1 lift, and calculated that it takes about 280K gas to verify

80
00:13:39.950 --> 00:13:53.310
Stefano Gogioso: one of these proofs, one of the SP1 lifts. So that… that at least is a full-stack, end-to-end verification that this, in principle, could be done. Now, of course, SP1 is not quantum, so that's not really

81
00:13:53.390 --> 00:13:58.330
Stefano Gogioso: That's not the endpoint, but nonetheless, it's a full run, all the way through.

82
00:13:58.490 --> 00:14:05.839
Stefano Gogioso: The architecture is two-stage. It's a secure element zkBoo that is…

83
00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:12.190
Stefano Gogioso: ZK, but not succeed. It runs in time, which is linear in the trace length.

84
00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:23.500
Stefano Gogioso: It also has memory, which is proportional with a small factor to the required memory, and so if you have a statement that can be run on the device with enough headroom, then you can run zkBoo on that statement on the device as well.

85
00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:36.869
Stefano Gogioso: It is also streamable, it can be streamed to a host machine. The host machine does not need to be trusted, because the zero knowledge is on a per-response basis, and so each response can just be sent through. It doesn't reveal anything about the secret on the device.

86
00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:50.919
Stefano Gogioso: But the host machine can have a leaf more horsepower, and can be used to re-verify the proof, and then wrap it into a succinct proof system, which, by the way, doesn't need to be zero-knowledge, so you don't have to ask for zK on the host.

87
00:14:51.140 --> 00:15:08.509
Stefano Gogioso: It can be parallelized over responses, it can be fully entrusted, it can be outsourced to third party, so that's an interesting division, but that's also where the challenges lie, because this, the wrapped, lifted proof, if you will, it becomes quite complicated, because it has to wrap the entire zkBoo, proof statement.

88
00:15:09.110 --> 00:15:10.660
Stefano Gogioso: zkBoo itself.

89
00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:17.870
Stefano Gogioso: It's NBC in the head, it has linear… well, XORA fine gates, is what it considers.

90
00:15:17.900 --> 00:15:34.999
Stefano Gogioso: ends with constants, source with constants, NOC, source, all of that is free, essentially. The only things you pay are the ends and the carries, which are ends. So that's… the size of the proof is really proportional to the number of end gates and carry gates in your circuit and nothing else, which is good.

91
00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:45.870
Stefano Gogioso: You then take independent responses, which you could, in principle parallelize, and then you run, sort of, you wrap them through Fiat Shamir, and you bind the message into that, and that is the proof.

92
00:15:45.910 --> 00:16:02.149
Stefano Gogioso: So the proof is the fact that the responses were ultimately computed for challenges which are derived from the absorbed, sort of the hashed, absorbed state at the end of the proofs, which is how this works. You absorb everything first, and then you derive the challenges along the way.

93
00:16:02.380 --> 00:16:04.280
Stefano Gogioso: Or that's… that's the trick.

94
00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:13.530
Stefano Gogioso: there are 3… there was a leaf of engineering that went into fitting this into LedgerFlex. It was hard.

95
00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:28.150
Stefano Gogioso: We had to improve quite significantly on implementation, so zkBoo. In fact, we had to build an entire zkBoo, crate ecosystem targeted to, sort of, non-standard, low-memory environments with all this optimization.

96
00:16:28.150 --> 00:16:41.760
Stefano Gogioso: Not going through all the details, but the basic sort of points. We don't actually store the trace at all, we just go through the computation, and you only need the registers for the largest intermediate state, so that's what you need to allocate, and that's it, so you can predict

97
00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:55.679
Stefano Gogioso: by a dry run, how much memory you will need for the entire proof. You never materialize any circuit, it's basically just the Rust program that runs through the gates, but you don't actually write the circuit in its extent. That would not fit on a ledger.

98
00:16:55.980 --> 00:17:12.010
Stefano Gogioso: There is, you regenerate the seed from a master secret, so the challenge phase does not have any buffering. You pay a little bit of extra time, but you reduce memory very significantly to the point that you can actually fit it on Ledger Flex.

99
00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:14.480
Stefano Gogioso: And you stream everything.

100
00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:26.389
Stefano Gogioso: you don't keep any of the responses in memory, you don't keep any of the… only the single message that you have computed, you stream it down out of the device as soon as it's produced, and then it's for the host to collect and assemble.

101
00:17:26.390 --> 00:17:41.240
Stefano Gogioso: And this means that the RAM required is around 3 times the largest circuit state, which makes it feasible, and the verifier RAM is actually two times the circuit state, because in principle, you can also do stream verification, which is something that we did implement as well.

102
00:17:42.390 --> 00:17:59.570
Stefano Gogioso: There are two soundness levels that we consider. We work with 438 responses, because that's the 128-bit post-quantum that's taking into account the possibility of Grover. But in principle, you… if you want just classical 128-bit, that is 219, so you could make this quite a leaf.

103
00:17:59.630 --> 00:18:19.620
Stefano Gogioso: faster. The verifier does not really care about this. It's completely independent of the chosen soundness level. The soundness level is just something that you check at the end. You just check that the proof has the right number of responses in it for your choice of… so, for your own threshold. There is no… it doesn't have any impact on the verification, because it's essentially massively parallel over the responses anyway.

104
00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:35.459
Stefano Gogioso: The secure element itself, we did a leaf of work on trying to figure out how the Ledger Flex and other Ledger devices behave. It is about 334K,

105
00:18:35.730 --> 00:18:48.759
Stefano Gogioso: of a stack plus heap that needs to be split and allocated depending on the application, so there's a… there's a level that you need to choose beforehand when you compile, and you have to respect that. There is…

106
00:18:48.970 --> 00:19:04.429
Stefano Gogioso: We did this on Ketchak, so that doesn't have any hardware acceleration, and that makes it slightly sore, but it doesn't really… I mean, at this point, the biggest constraint is not really the hashing, the biggest constraint is the fact that the I.O. of the device is quite limited.

107
00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:11.679
Stefano Gogioso: The… these are the results in terms of response sizes and timing.

108
00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:18.230
Stefano Gogioso: For a master private key, the response size is about 50, kilobytes.

109
00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:24.980
Stefano Gogioso: And it takes about 30 seconds per response on the device. That's the same for a hardened child.

110
00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:30.260
Stefano Gogioso: And it's similar for one or two rounds of the seed derivation.

111
00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:49.330
Stefano Gogioso: If you go to the public key and ethereum address and the normal non-Hargon child key, then that involves elliptic curve operations, and as I said, it becomes much, much, much, much larger to do so. But nonetheless, it's something that you can do within a few days. So it takes days to generate a proof for just the public.

112
00:19:49.400 --> 00:19:59.700
Stefano Gogioso: key, rather than a private key that you can burn. And the argument for why you would not want to do that in a real emergency is that your private key in an emergency is worthless anyway.

113
00:20:00.170 --> 00:20:12.519
Stefano Gogioso: So, once you have a situation where anybody can derive your private key and pretend to be you, then that private key doesn't really hold any secrecy anymore, and you might as well burn it in exchange for,

114
00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:23.950
Stefano Gogioso: cheaper on-chain proof and for a faster proof generation on-device, but nonetheless, we wanted to benchmark the case where you wouldn't want to derive that. For example, you want to create a proof today.

115
00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:29.349
Stefano Gogioso: not necessarily commit… you could create one today, commit a hash of the, of the…

116
00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:39.560
Stefano Gogioso: of the private key, and then unlock the smart contract, the account structure, by revealing the family key and burning it, start the private key and burning it. You could do that, in principle.

117
00:20:39.560 --> 00:20:49.749
Stefano Gogioso: But, if you wanted to instead just do a direct proof that you are the owner of a public address, that's also of interest, then we did that. But it's much larger because of these operations.

118
00:20:50.910 --> 00:21:09.669
Stefano Gogioso: The… yeah, it takes about 3 hours to do a master key of hardened child statement on device. That's about 22 megabytes of proof. We did projections for shorter runs for the full curve statements and the BIP39 statements. The curve statements take about a week.

119
00:21:09.980 --> 00:21:27.710
Stefano Gogioso: And they are one gigabyte of proof. Again, this can be stream aggregated, so it's not that big of a deal that it's 1 gig, but it does take more than 7 days to stream it out of the device, because the I.O. is constrained. If you want to derive the seed from the

120
00:21:27.710 --> 00:21:33.389
Stefano Gogioso: Well, if you want to write the master entropy from the seed phrase, which is what gives the name to this project.

121
00:21:33.390 --> 00:21:48.419
Stefano Gogioso: at the full 2048 rounds, that is 150 days. So that is hard. But, it's still feasible in principle. It does fit the memory, it's just a matter of time. You just have to be very, very patient. But in a pinch, you could do that.

122
00:21:49.020 --> 00:22:06.439
Stefano Gogioso: the per response, changed quite a leaf. We did a leaf of work on this. We went from, 1 gig per response on, when we presented at EthCC, that's when I called these things, like, elephants. They were really impractical.

123
00:22:06.440 --> 00:22:19.270
Stefano Gogioso: We applied quite a leaf of optimizations in, sort of, like, some… during the summer, and it turns out that once you do it the smart way, the per response size is actually 3 megabits.

124
00:22:19.330 --> 00:22:39.229
Stefano Gogioso: So that's 300 times smaller. So it was just that I said it in its ZCC when I was presented this, I said, yeah, okay, it looks huge, but I'm pretty sure it can be optimized, and yes, it can be optimized 300X, so that's great. It's still big, but better than… one gig per response was absolutely unfeasible. It was, like, 438 weeks to do the entire thing.

125
00:22:39.230 --> 00:22:42.940
Stefano Gogioso: now it takes about a week. So that's… that's a nice input.

126
00:22:44.290 --> 00:22:47.199
Stefano Gogioso: Memory itself, it was the main challenge.

127
00:22:47.620 --> 00:23:03.939
Stefano Gogioso: at the beginning, it's the reason why we had to re-implement everything. It turns out it is not a constraint for any of these. All of them fit within the maximum heap that we could afford for it, and heap plus stack fit within the memory. We tested all of them, that's fine.

128
00:23:03.940 --> 00:23:07.809
Stefano Gogioso: the only thing that I want to mention there is

129
00:23:07.910 --> 00:23:21.360
Stefano Gogioso: That this was a bit irritating to do, because the typical consequence of getting the memory allocation wrong is not that the application fails, but it's that the device gets wiped.

130
